21:04:26 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That is a very fair point. Sending a machine is an incredible feat of engineering, but you are absolutely right—it is fundamentally different from a human being actually stepping out of a spacecraft and walking on another world. If we strictly mean **human beings**, the reason we have walked on the Moon but not on another planet comes down to a massive leap in difficulty. Going to the Moon is like camping in your backyard; going to Mars (the closest planet we could realistically land on) is like trying to survive an expedition to Antarctica. Here is exactly why humans haven't made it to another planet yet: **1. The Tyranny of Distance and Time** The Moon is right next door in cosmic terms. It takes about **three days** to get there. If something goes wrong, as it did on Apollo 13, there is a realistic chance of turning around and getting the crew home alive. Mars, on the other hand, is millions of miles away. A trip to Mars takes **seven to nine months just to get there**. Because Earth and Mars orbit the sun at different speeds, astronauts would have to wait on Mars for over a year before the planets aligned properly for a trip home. That means a Mars mission is a **two to three-year commitment**. **2. The Packing List (Life Support)** For a three-year trip, you have to bring all the food, water, oxygen, and medical supplies the crew will need for the entire duration, because there is no way to resupply them. The sheer weight of all those supplies requires a massive, incredibly expensive rocket. **3. The Return Trip is a Nightmare** Getting humans to Mars is only half the battle; getting them back is the hardest part. The Moon has very little gravity, so the Apollo astronauts could take off in a tiny, lightweight lunar module. Mars has much stronger gravity. To get astronauts off the surface of Mars and back to Earth, they would need a full-sized rocket. That means we either have to launch a fully fueled rocket from Earth and land it on 